#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:0
msgid ""
"Understand the software and hardware layers behind Ubuntu Desktop "
"accessibility, including AT-SPI, GTK, Orca, BRLTTY, USB and Bluetooth."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:7
msgid "Topics: Accessibility"
msgstr "話題：無障礙功能"

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:11
msgid "Accessibility stack"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:13
msgid "Software interfaces for assistive technologies"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:15
msgid ""
"This section provides details on the components that make up Ubuntu "
"Desktop's accessibility stack, their roles, dependencies, and how they all "
"work together. There are also recommendations for developers and integrators "
"who want to create applications that will work with Desktop's default "
"assistive solutions as well as developers who want to create their own "
"assistive solutions that would work on Desktop."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:17
msgid "AT-SPI"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:19
msgid ""
"The Assistive Technology Service Provider Interface (AT-SPI) is a platform-"
"neutral framework for communication between assistive technologies (AT) and "
"applications."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:21
msgid ""
"AT-SPI defines a set of interfaces for assistive technologies which allow to "
"get names, roles, and states of interface objects, listen for events, or "
"perform actions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:23
msgid ""
"AT-SPI uses D-Bus method calls and signals. D-Bus is an inter-process "
"communication system in Linux, which makes AT-SPI a default accessibility "
"framework for Linux."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:25
msgid "AT-SPI is made of two components:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:27
msgid "`at-spi2-core`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:28
msgid "A collection of XML interfaces for accessibility"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:30
msgid "`at-spi2-atk`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:31
msgid ""
"A library that allows applications made with GTK 3 to register accessible "
"objects with AT-SPI"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:33
msgid ""
"If you are building custom assistive technology tools or want to test the "
"accessibility of custom applications that were not built with GTK on Ubuntu "
"Desktop, you must use {ref}`at-spi-dbus-xml-interfaces`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:35
msgid "GTK4 and Gtk.Accessible"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:37
msgid ""
"GTK is a cross-platform widget toolkit for creating graphical user "
"interfaces. It offers a set of user interface elements that allow developers "
"to build apps quickly and in a consistent manner. The majority of "
"applications within GNOME desktop environment are built with GTK."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:39
msgid ""
"GTK4 is a newer version of GTK that implements its own [Gtk.Accessible]"
"(https://docs.gtk.org/gtk4/iface.Accessible.html) interface instead of the "
"previously used ATK."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:41
msgid ""
"If you are building an application for Ubuntu Desktop and want to make it "
"accessible for Ubuntu Desktop's default assistive technologies such as Orca, "
"it is recommended that you use GTK. Desktop's assistive solutions are able "
"to access information about the apps built with both GTK3 and GTK4."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:43
msgid ""
"If you are using a different UI toolkit, you must ensure it implements the "
"AT-SPI D-Bus interfaces; otherwise, Desktop's assistive technologies "
"wouldn't be able to interact with it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:45
msgid "GTK3 and ATK"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:47
msgid ""
"GTK3 version of the GTK toolkit that integrates with Accessibility Toolkit "
"(ATK). Applications built with GTK3 expose information about UI objects "
"using the ATK. ATK is an Application Programming Interface (API) that allows "
"developers to avoid working with AT-SPI API directly, as it provides a set "
"of special input methods."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:7
#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:49
msgid "Flutter"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:51
msgid ""
"A selection of applications on Ubuntu Desktop such as [App Center](https://"
"github.com/ubuntu/app-center) are built with Flutter. Flutter engine has "
"compatibility issues with AT-SPI. To learn how to make Flutter applications "
"that would be compatible with Ubuntu Desktop's default assistive solutions, "
"reach out to the development team at [Discourse](https://"
"discourse.ubuntu.com/tag/flutter)."
msgstr ""

