#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:65
msgid "Screen reader and speech synthesis"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:67
msgid ""
"Speech synthesizers converts text to human-sounding speech. Typically, they "
"are offered as a backend for screen readers, but they can also be used as "
"standalone programs. Orca is the default screen reader on Ubuntu Desktop. "
"eSpeak and Speech Dispatcher are the  speech synthesizers that Orca depends "
"on. When Orca identifies the text, it passes it to the Speech Dispatcher for "
"speech synthesis; the Speech Dispatcher then passes the synthesized speech "
"to eSpeak, which plays the sound."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:70
msgid "Orca"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:72
msgid "Orca is a screen reader for vision impaired and blind users."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:74
msgid ""
"Orca is preinstalled on Ubuntu Desktop and can be used out-of-box. To learn "
"how to configure it, go to {ref}`read-screen-aloud`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:76
msgid "BRLTTY"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:78
msgid ""
"The Orca screen reader can display the user interface on a refreshable "
"Braille display. It uses the BRLTTY service, which provides access to the "
"Linux console for a blind person using a refreshable Braille display."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:80
msgid ""
"As Orca, BRLTTY is preinstalled and can be enabled and used without any "
"additional configurations, see {ref}`read-screen-in-braille`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:82
msgid "eSpeak"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:84
msgid ""
"eSpeak is a speech synthesizer that supports over 100 languages and accents. "
"eSpeak can be used as a standalone tool or as a backend component for screen "
"readers, see [eSpeak website](https://espeak.sourceforge.net/)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:86
msgid "Speech dispatcher"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:88
msgid ""
"Speech dispatcher is a device-independent high-level interface for speech "
"synthesis. It can be used by other software and hardware speech synthesizers "
"as a backend, see [Speech Dispatcher website](https://freebsoft.org/speechd)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:90
msgid ""
"Therefore, Speech Dispatcher is the layer that allows you to switch between "
"text-to-speech (TTS) solutions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:92
msgid "On-screen keyboards"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:94
msgid "GNOME on-screen keyboard"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:96
msgid ""
"Ubuntu Desktop comes with a GNOME on-screen keyboard preinstalled, it is the "
"on-screen keyboard that we recommend for Desktop. To start using it, see "
"{ref}`use-an-on-screen-keyboard`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:101
msgid "Hardware interfaces for assistive technologies"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:103
msgid "USB"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:105
msgid "Ubuntu Desktop supports USB out of the box."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:107
msgid ""
"In Ubuntu, kernel USB drivers handle communications with USB devices such "
"as, for example, `usbhid` which provides support for USB Human Interface "
"Devices (HID) class. Kernel supports all [standard USB classes](https://"
"www.usb.org/defined-class-codes)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:109
msgid ""
"`udev` is a device manager in Ubuntu that detects, adds and removes devices. "
"`udev` provides access to the detected USB devices in the `/dev` directory."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:111
msgid ""
"If your device conforms to a USB class, it will be assigned a `/dev` entry "
"and recognized correctly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:113
msgid "USB device requirements"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:115
msgid ""
"You can use USB to connect assistive devices for input/output such as "
"Braille displays, sip-and-puff, foot pedals."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:117
msgid "To be able to work on Ubuntu properly, a USB device must:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:119
msgid "use a standard USB class"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:120
msgid "provide a proper USB descriptor so that Ubuntu can identify it"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:121
msgid "not require proprietary drivers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:123
msgid ""
"If the device uses a custom class, it must provide a `udev` `.rules` file "
"instead so that Ubuntu can identify it correctly. See [`udev` documentation]"
"(https://www.man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/udev.7.html)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:125
msgid "Bluetooth"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:127
msgid ""
"While the Ubuntu Desktop team works continuously to enhance the Bluetooth "
"experience, the Bluetooth stack involves a complex interplay of multiple "
"components such as firmware, kernel modules, drivers, and user-space "
"applications. This complex set of dependencies makes it difficult to "
"guarantee consistent compatibility with all Bluetooth devices. For an in-"
"depth explanation of Ubuntu's hardware architecture, see {ref}`hardware-"
"stack`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:129
msgid "Ubuntu uses BlueZ as its core Bluetooth stack."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:131
msgid "To be able to work on Desktop, a device must:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:133
msgid "be HCI-compliant"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:134
msgid ""
"have a corresponding firmware provided by the manufacturer as part of the "
"`linux-firmware` package"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:135
msgid "use the [profiles supported by BlueZ](https://www.bluez.org/profiles/)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:137
msgid "See also"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../explanation/accessibility-stack.md:139
msgid "{ref}`troubleshoot-bluetooth`"
msgstr ""
